#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175754 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "acac66ab-ff2a-5727-8a2c-9cce3e18806c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9036" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2xy - x^2 - y^2$.\nUsing a substitution of the form $z= x - y$, transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z'(x) = f(z)$. What is the function $f(z)$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= - (x - y)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n$z = x - y$.\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - \\frac{dz}{dx} &= -z^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form\n\n$$\nz'(x) = f(z)\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = z^2 + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(z) = z^2 + 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175755 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "37a41ca0-2205-5368-91ec-d9a4c1a309d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9038" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1 - x - y}{x + y}$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n$g(x,y) = 2x + C$,\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (1 - (x + y))/(x + y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - 1 &= \\frac{1 - z}{z}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form\n\n$$\nz'(x) = f(z)\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{z}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz\\frac{dz}{dx} &= 1 \\\\\n∫zdz &= ∫dx \\\\\n\\frac{z^{2}}{2} &= x + K \\\\\nz^{2} &= 2x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 2K$.\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 = 2x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = (x+y)^2\n$$"}]}
